WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.435
Cost per successful task, and latency as a constraint.

2
00:00:03.715 --> 00:00:06.872
Price per million tokens is an input, not an answer.

3
00:00:07.152 --> 00:00:19.013
What you pay for is completed work: cost per successful task is total spend (input, output and thinking tokens, retries, any judge calls) divided by the number of tasks that passed.

4
00:00:19.293 --> 00:00:27.848
A model with a third of the price per token can cost more per success if it uses more tokens, needs more retries, or fails more often.

5
00:00:28.128 --> 00:00:37.387
Measure from usage records on your own tasks rather than computing from list prices, since token counts per task differ across models and tokenizers.

6
00:00:37.667 --> 00:00:39.949
Then include what failure costs downstream.

7
00:00:40.229 --> 00:00:44.859
If a failed answer is caught and sent to a person, add the review cost per failure.

8
00:00:45.139 --> 00:00:53.352
If it reaches a customer, the cost is harder to price, and that is usually the argument for a higher quality bar rather than a cheaper model.

9
00:00:53.632 --> 00:01:10.784
For self-hosted open-weight models, there is no per-token fee, but the cost moves to GPUs (amortized over expected volume, including idle time), operations, and staff time; a fair comparison prices it per successful task at your realistic traffic.

10
00:01:11.064 --> 00:01:22.243
Latency usually works as a constraint rather than a score: the product needs p95 under some number of seconds, and configurations above it are out regardless of quality.

11
00:01:22.523 --> 00:01:28.709
Measure percentiles from your region, at your prompt sizes, with streaming on or off as the product uses it.

12
00:01:28.989 --> 00:01:39.912
Independent services such as Artificial Analysis publish speed and latency measurements across providers’ endpoints, which are useful for a shortlist; your own measurements decide.

13
00:01:40.192 --> 00:01:48.107
With quality, cost, and latency in hand, plot each configuration as a point with pass rate up and cost per task to the right.

14
00:01:48.387 --> 00:01:55.384
The Pareto frontier is the set of configurations that no other configuration beats on both axes at once.

15
00:01:55.664 --> 00:01:58.971
Anything off the frontier is dominated and can be dropped.

16
00:01:59.251 --> 00:02:04.840
The choice along the frontier is a business decision: how much is another point of quality worth?
